WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.825
The assessment report should never
claim coverage it doesn't have,

2
00:00:03.825 --> 00:00:07.462
so you need to know exactly where
the default read boundary sits.

3
00:00:07.462 --> 00:00:11.113
Four mechanisms set it, and
several are commonly misstated.

4
00:00:11.113 --> 00:00:13.200
Protected accounts and groups.

5
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:18.367
The AdminSDHolder object holds template
permissions for Domain Admins,

6
00:00:18.367 --> 00:00:23.450
Enterprise Admins, Schema Admins, the
operator groups, krbtgt and others.

7
00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:27.064
A process called S D Prop
runs every sixty minutes by

8
00:00:27.064 --> 00:00:31.350
default on the PDC emulator and
resets those objects to match.

9
00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:33.288
It acts on current members only.

10
00:00:33.288 --> 00:00:36.181
An account removed from
a protected group keeps

11
00:00:36.181 --> 00:00:39.001
admin count set to one
unless someone clears it.

12
00:00:39.001 --> 00:00:42.495
So admin count lists accounts
that are or were privileged:

13
00:00:42.495 --> 00:00:46.062
a good clean-up list, not the
current privileged population.

14
00:00:46.062 --> 00:00:47.787
Confidential attributes.

15
00:00:47.787 --> 00:00:50.749
Setting bit seven, value
one hundred twenty-eight,

16
00:00:50.749 --> 00:00:54.649
of an attribute's search flags
means reading it needs the control

17
00:00:54.649 --> 00:00:58.476
access right as well, which by
default only administrators hold.

18
00:00:58.476 --> 00:01:02.907
You can still show where a capability
is deployed without seeing the secret:

19
00:01:02.907 --> 00:01:05.511
with Windows LAPS, the
presence and age of the

20
00:01:05.511 --> 00:01:08.325
password expiration
attribute shows coverage.

21
00:01:08.325 --> 00:01:12.504
Legacy Microsoft LAPS is
deprecated as of Windows 11 23H2,

22
00:01:12.504 --> 00:01:18.337
so a forest still on it has a finding before
the assessment even reaches identity.

23
00:01:18.337 --> 00:01:19.313
Secrets.

24
00:01:19.313 --> 00:01:23.413
Password hashes leave a domain
controller only through replication,

25
00:01:23.413 --> 00:01:28.362
which needs Replicating Directory Changes
and Replicating Directory Changes All.

26
00:01:28.362 --> 00:01:30.674
The assessment never requests those.

27
00:01:30.674 --> 00:01:33.978
But which accounts already hold
them is a key later check:

28
00:01:33.978 --> 00:01:38.687
any holder that isn't a domain controller
or the sync service account is a finding.

29
00:01:38.687 --> 00:01:40.338
Restricted containers.

30
00:01:40.338 --> 00:01:44.676
Deleted Objects is visible only
to System and Administrators.

31
00:01:44.676 --> 00:01:49.050
Restoring needs Reanimate Tombstone,
which an assessment never needs.

32
00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:52.498
Fine-grained password policies
belong to Domain Admins,

33
00:01:52.498 --> 00:01:55.725
so record them as not seen
rather than inferring them.

34
00:01:55.725 --> 00:01:58.630
Finally, report only measured coverage.
